{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"05","chapter_title":"Productivity and Workflow Automation","title":"Automation Safety Checks","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting automation safety checks to automation safety record, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-01","statement":"Analyze how the automation safety record shows a defensible decision in CAT 100"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-04","statement":"Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it"}],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation","cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":5,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/07/04"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_calibration","cat100-supplement_07_05_lo_02_direct_check"],"lo_practice_map":{"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-01":["cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_calibration"],"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-02":["cat100-supplement_07_05_lo_02_direct_check"],"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-03":["cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_ai_boundary"],"cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-04":["cat100-supplement_07_05_lo_02_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Automation Safety Checks\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Automation Safety Checks, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; all rights reserved pending project license decision\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/07-05-automation-safety-checks.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Automation Safety Checks: Automation Safety Checks, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nAutomation is a force multiplier, and a force multiplier multiplies mistakes as readily as it multiplies good work. A rename rule, a bulk delete, a mail filter, or a find-and-replace applied to 200 items will execute a flawed pattern 200 times in a second, with no pause to let you notice. The thing that makes automation safe is not better luck; it is a habit of testing on a small sample first and keeping an undo path for actions the tool cannot reverse. This section teaches that habit: dry run, backup, then scale.\n\nThe stakes are concrete. A wrong rename pattern can corrupt every file name in a folder; a bad bulk delete can empty a drive past the point a recycle bin can recover. The safety check costs a minute; the missing safety check can cost a semester of work.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Run any bulk operation on a small sample (a dry run) before applying it to everything.\n- Create a backup or undo path before any action the tool cannot reverse.\n- Confirm the sample result is correct before scaling, so the full run is verification, not a gamble.\n- Distinguish reversible actions (safe to try) from irreversible ones (must be backed up first).\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA student wants to add a date prefix to 200 coursework files and writes a rename rule. The pattern has a subtle flaw: it prepends the date to a name that already starts with the date, but the student does not see this, because they run it on all 200 files at once. In one click, every file becomes `2026-05-28_2026-05-28_essay.docx`, and the rename tool offers no single-step undo. The error was small; the blast radius was the entire folder.\n\nThe fix is structural, not careful: test the rule on three copies first, confirm the output, then run it on the full set with a backup already in place.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAutomation safety rests on two ideas: limiting the blast radius and preserving reversibility.\n\n**Blast radius** is how many items a single action affects. The danger of automation is that the blast radius equals the whole set, so one flawed rule damages everything at once. A **dry run** shrinks the blast radius to a tiny sample: copy three files into a test folder, run the rule there, and inspect the result. If the pattern is wrong, only three throwaway copies are affected, and you learn it for free.\n\n**Reversibility** is whether you can get back to the prior state. Some actions are reversible (moving a file, which you can move back). Many automated actions are not: a permanent delete, an overwrite, or a rename with no built-in undo. For any irreversible action, you make a **backup** first, which manufactures an undo path the tool itself does not provide.\n\nThe procedure that follows from these ideas is fixed:\n\n1. Copy a small sample into a test location.\n2. Run the operation on the sample only.\n3. Inspect the sample output against what you expected.\n4. Back up the full set (the undo path).\n5. Run on the full set, treating it as verification, not a first try.\n\nThe common error is collapsing this to a single step (run on everything and hope), which works until the day it does not, and that day is unrecoverable.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A rename rule will touch 200 files at once and the tool offers no undo button. Before clicking run, which single safeguard most reduces the blast radius if the pattern is subtly wrong?\" explanation=\"A dry run on a small sample shrinks the blast radius from 200 files to three throwaway copies, so a flawed pattern is caught for free. Reading the rule again cannot reveal output errors, and a faster machine only applies the wrong pattern faster.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Copy three files into a test folder and run the rule there first\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Read the rename rule one more time to be sure it looks right\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Run it on a faster machine so it finishes before any damage spreads\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Trust the recycle bin to recover anything that goes wrong\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nHere is a real bulk action made safe with a dry run and an undo path.\n\n**The task:** add a date prefix to 200 coursework files with a rename rule.\n\n**Unsafe (run on everything at once):**\n\n```\nRule:  rename *  ->  2026-05-28_*\nRun on:  CAT100/ (all 200 files)\nResult:  pattern was wrong; 200 files now read 2026-05-28_2026-05-28_essay.docx\n```\n\nOne bad pattern just corrupted every file.\n\n**Safe (dry run on copies, then full run with a backup):**\n\n```\n1. Copy 3 files into CAT100/_test/        (sample)\n2. Run rule on _test/ only\n   essay.docx        -> 2026-05-28_essay.docx     OK\n   lab.docx          -> 2026-05-28_lab.docx        OK\n3. Backup CAT100/ to OneDrive              (undo path)\n4. Run rule on the full folder\n```\n\nThree safety checks did the work:\n\n1. **Dry run on a small sample** caught the pattern before it touched all 200 files.\n2. **A backup copy** in OneDrive gave an undo path for an action the tool cannot reverse.\n3. **Confirm the result on the sample** before scaling, so the full run is verification, not a gamble.\n\nThe safe procedure has a fixed order. Drag these five moves into the sequence that limits the blast radius and preserves an undo path.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Copy a small sample (three files) into a separate test location\",\"Run the operation on the sample only, never on the live set\",\"Inspect the sample output against exactly what you expected\",\"Back up the full set to create the undo path the tool does not provide\",\"Run on the full set, treating it as verification of an already-tested rule\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nBefore running any operation on more than a handful of items, ask: can I test this on a small sample first, and if it goes wrong, can I undo it? If the answer to either is no, do not run it yet; create the test or the backup first.\n\nA safe bulk operation passes three checks:\n\n1. **Dry-run check:** Was the operation tested on a small sample and the output inspected before scaling?\n2. **Reversibility check:** Is there a backup or undo path for any action the tool cannot itself reverse?\n3. **Confirm-before-scale check:** Did the sample produce exactly the expected result before the full run?\n\nIf you cannot point to the test sample and the backup, the full run is a gamble, not a safe operation.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common misreading is \"I read the rule carefully, so it is fine.\" Careful reading does not catch subtle pattern errors; a dry run does. Inspection of actual output beats inspection of the rule.\n\nA second misreading is assuming the recycle bin or an app's undo button is a sufficient safety net. Many bulk tools bypass the recycle bin or offer no undo at all, which is exactly why you make your own backup before running.\n\n**In real tools (2026).** Each safeguard has a concrete home in current tools. For bulk renames, Windows PowerToys PowerRename shows a live preview of every new name before you commit (a built-in dry run) and can undo a completed rename; macOS Finder's batch Rename previews the result as well. For undo paths, OneDrive and Google Drive both keep version history (around 30 days of file restores on personal accounts). For email rules, Outlook and Gmail filters let you review what a rule matched and adjust it. These interfaces change, so the live tool, not a remembered screenshot, is the source of truth.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply this to a bulk delete, the most dangerous common operation. You want to clear out old downloads: delete every file in `Downloads/` older than 90 days. The risk is that an important PDF you saved months ago also matches \"older than 90 days.\" Run the dry run first by sorting the folder by date and listing what the rule would catch, without deleting; scan that list and you spot the syllabus you meant to keep.\n\nNow adjust the rule (exclude PDFs, or move-to-archive instead of delete), back up `Downloads/` to the cloud as the undo path, and only then run the deletion. The walkthrough shows that the dry run is not a formality: it surfaces the false matches (files that fit the rule but should not be touched) that a careful reading of the rule would miss, and the backup covers the case where you miss one anyway.\n\nWork the bulk-delete case one decision at a time, predicting each safeguard before it is revealed.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Make a ’delete everything older than 90 days’ rule safe\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You plan to delete every file in Downloads older than 90 days. A permanent bulk delete is the operation. What do you do before deleting anything?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Delete immediately to reclaim space\",\"Sort by date and list what the rule would catch, without deleting (a dry run)\",\"Empty the recycle bin first to make room\",\"Delete and check the results afterward\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Dry run first. Listing the matches without deleting lets you scan for false matches, and you spot the syllabus PDF you saved months ago that also fits ’older than 90 days.’\"},{\"prompt\":\"The dry-run list includes a PDF you need. The rule has a false match. What is the better next move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Delete anyway; one file is acceptable loss\",\"Refine the rule (exclude PDFs, or move-to-archive instead of delete)\",\"Lower the age threshold to 30 days and delete more\",\"Run it twice to be thorough\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Fix the false match. Excluding PDFs or moving to an archive folder instead of deleting removes the file you needed from the blast radius.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The refined rule looks right and the action is a permanent delete. What is the last safeguard before running it?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Nothing; the dry run was enough\",\"Back up Downloads to the cloud as an undo path, then run\",\"Trust the recycle bin to catch mistakes\",\"Run it and watch closely\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Back up for irreversibility. A permanent delete has no built-in undo, so a cloud backup of Downloads covers the case where the refined rule still misses something.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Dry run surfaced the false match, the refined rule removed it from the blast radius, and the backup covered the residual risk. The same dry-run-then-backup habit makes every irreversible bulk action survivable.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats every automated action as guilty until proven safe, asking what the worst case is before running it. They mentally separate \"this is reversible\" from \"this is permanent\" and apply heavier safeguards to the permanent ones.\n\nA strong reader of an automated operation can answer four questions:\n\n1. How many items will this affect, and what is the worst thing that happens if the rule is wrong?\n2. Did I test it on a sample and inspect the actual output, not just the rule?\n3. Is this action reversible, and if not, where is the backup?\n4. Which items match the rule but should not be touched, and how did I catch them?\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks\", \"cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is a record of a bulk operation done safely: the rule, the dry-run sample and its output, the backup location, and the full-run result. It is strong when a reader can see that the operation was tested and reversible before it ran at scale.\n\nA useful note sounds like this: \"I tested the rename rule on three copies first and caught a doubled-prefix bug; I fixed it, backed up the folder to OneDrive, then ran the full rename. I rejected running directly on the live folder even though it was faster, because the tool has no undo and a wrong pattern would have hit all 200 files.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\n**When the actor is an AI agent.** The same three checks apply when an AI assistant or agent proposes a bulk action (delete, move, send, overwrite), plus one more gate: require a dry-run preview of exactly what it would do before it acts, never grant an agent irreversible permissions without a current backup, and log the proposal in your accepted/modified/rejected note. A rejected bulk-delete suggestion, with the reason, is exactly the kind of entry that proves the judgment stayed with you.\n\nMove the safety habit from file renaming to a spreadsheet find-and-replace. Dry run, backup, and confirm-before-scale all transfer, but the test method changes: instead of copying files, you duplicate the sheet (or save a copy of the workbook) and run the replace there first, watching for unintended matches like replacing \"1\" inside \"10\" or \"100.\" The procedure is identical; what adapts is how you build the sample (a copied sheet rather than copied files) and what false matches to watch for.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the automation safety checks packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/07-05-automation-safety-checks-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"bulk operation safety record with dry run and backup\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"07.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the bulk operation safety record.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the dry-run-and-backup rule to a new setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the operation.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the automation safety record for CAT 100 07.05. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, confirm your operation passes the dry-run, reversibility, and confirm-before-scale checks: tested on a sample, backed up for irreversible actions, and the sample output verified before the full run.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- Microsoft Support: How to bypass the Recycle Bin when deleting files (Shift+Delete is permanent). https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/topic/how-to-bypass-the-recycle-bin-when-deleting-files-c343f9ef-1934-1bdb-9766-2d418d4cfe34\n- Microsoft Support: Restore a previous version of a file stored in OneDrive. https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/restore-a-previous-version-of-a-file-stored-in-onedrive-159cad6d-d76e-4981-88ef-de6e96c93893\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","cat100_supplement_productivity_and_workflow_automation"],"stem":"You wrote a rename rule to add a date prefix to 200 coursework files. What is the safe first step?","options":["Run it on all 200 at once to save time","Run it on a copy of two or three files, check the result, then run the full set","Run it twice to be sure","Rename the folder instead"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A small dry run on copies catches a bad pattern before it corrupts all 200 files.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_no_dry_run","difficulty":"easy","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks"],"stem":"In a spreadsheet you filter to show only 'overdue' rows, delete them, then clear the filter and find other rows missing. What went wrong, and what is safer?","options":["Filtering is never safe; never use it","Deleting on a live filter can hit unintended rows; copy the rows you want to keep to a new sheet, or sort instead, then delete","You should have deleted the columns","The spreadsheet needed to be a PDF"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Deleting filtered rows can affect more than what you see. Preserving the kept rows first makes the delete reviewable.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_filter_then_delete_all","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks"],"stem":"You set an email rule: 'if subject contains class, move to Archive.' A week later you miss an advisor message. What is the fix?","options":["Delete all your email rules","Make the rule specific (match the sender plus a precise subject) and check the Archive folder regularly","Move everything to Archive","Turn off email entirely"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An overbroad rule silently buries needed mail. Narrow the match and review the destination folder.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_inbox_rule_too_broad","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-03"]},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_automation_safety_checks_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_automation_safety_checks"],"stem":"An automation will overwrite your synced project file with a generated version and cannot be undone in the app. What must you confirm first?","options":["That the new file has a nicer name","That a backup copy exists and there is version history or an undo path to recover the original","That you run it on a Friday","That the file is under 1 MB"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Irreversible automations need a recovery path. A backup plus version history means a bad run is not permanent.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_no_undo_check","difficulty":"hard","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat100-supplement_07_05_lo_02_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-02","cat100-supplement-07-05-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_no_dry_run","pattern":"run_automation_on_real_data_first","description":"Running a bulk action like a mail merge or rename script on real files before testing on a small sample","trigger":"learner runs a rename macro on the whole folder and corrupts 200 files at once","feedback":"Test on a copy of two or three files first. Confirm the result, then run the automation on the full set.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_filter_then_delete_all","pattern":"delete_after_filter_without_clearing","description":"Deleting rows after a spreadsheet filter without realizing hidden rows can be affected or that the filter is still active","trigger":"learner filters to 'overdue', deletes, then finds the wrong rows gone when the filter clears","feedback":"Before deleting filtered rows, copy the kept rows to a new sheet, or sort instead of filter, so you can see exactly what goes.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_inbox_rule_too_broad","pattern":"overbroad_auto_rule","description":"Writing an email auto-rule so broad it sweeps important messages into a folder you never check","trigger":"a rule 'if contains class, move to Archive' also hides advisor and registrar mail","feedback":"Make rules specific (match sender plus subject) and review the target folder, so a rule never silently buries something you need.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_7_5_no_undo_check","pattern":"irreversible_action_no_backup","description":"Triggering an automation that cannot be undone without first making a backup or confirming an undo path","trigger":"learner empties trash or overwrites a synced file with no copy to recover","feedback":"Before an irreversible automation, save a backup copy and confirm there is an undo or version history to fall back on.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"b2af3fa7d3f28570763157a662f0b322c20acb19effc4bfae1786ddecfe54dea","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}